# Simulated alignments — ~750 MB, and reproducible. Regenerate with
# shell/generate_alignments.sh (needs wgsim, pbsim, minimap2, samtools), then
# shell/load_alignments.sh to wire them into every build under builds/.
# The small tracked inputs that sit alongside them in data/ — hg19mod.fa(.fai),
# hg19_17.chrom.sizes, R103.model — are not matched by these patterns.
data/*.bam
data/*.bam.bai
data/*.cram
data/*.cram.crai
data/*.bw

# Gene/transcript/exon GFF3 for the ecosystem GFF3 benchmark, ~60 MB.
# Regenerate with shell/generate_gff3.sh, which needs nothing but node — the
# records come from a seeded RNG, so every machine gets byte-identical files.
# Generated rather than cut from a real annotation for a second reason too:
# concatenating a real GFF3 to size it up does not work, because ids repeat
# across the copies and every duplicate child attaches to the first parent
# carrying its id, deepening the tree instead of lengthening the file.
data/features.*.gff3

# Multi-sample VCFs for the ecosystem VCF benchmark, ~37 MB. Regenerate with
# shell/generate_variants.sh, which needs nothing but node — the records come
# from a seeded RNG, so every machine gets byte-identical files.
data/variants.*.vcf

# The row-sweep corpus: the same callset at six sample counts, bgzipped and
# tabix indexed so the browser can range-request it. Regenerate with
# shell/generate_rowsweep.sh (node, bgzip, tabix), then shell/load_rowsweep.js
# to register the tracks in a staged build.
data/rowsweep/

# The cohort signal panel: one BigWig per sample over the benchmark contig, for
# ecosystem/cohort-bw.ts. Regenerate with shell/generate_cohort_bw.sh (node,
# bedGraphToBigWig) — a few MB and under a minute for the default 100.
data/cohort/

# The 2019 cram-js paper's own corpus: two 1000 Genomes NA12878 CRAMs, GRCh38
# with decoys, and the E. coli fixture — ~16 GB, downloaded rather than
# generated. Fetch with shell/fetch_paper2019.sh, which is resumable and skips
# anything already at full size. Nothing else in this repo needs it; only
# ecosystem/cram-samtools.ts reads it, and that runs without it.
data/paper2019/

# Per-run logs from the top-level Makefile. The tables and JSON they produced
# are tracked; the console transcript behind them is not, except where a run was
# interesting enough to be committed by hand (results/run-*.log).
results/logs/

# jbrowse-web builds under test. Each is a deployed build plus symlinked copies
# of the alignment data; stage them per README ("Builds compared").
builds/

node_modules/

# V8 CPU profiles from flameprofile.ts / interaction-profile.ts, ~600 KB each.
# The conclusions drawn from them are in flame/*.md, which are tracked.
*.cpuprofile

# Puppeteer verify/probe output
screenshots/

# Top-level logs from building and generating; results/*.log is tracked, since
# those are the raw per-run numbers behind the tables.
/build_webgl.log
/gen_alignments.log

# Derived flamegraph intermediates (from *.cpuprofile, which is ignored)
flame/*.folded
flame/*.svg

# A superseded backend table. scripts/render/backends.ts writes one file per
# browser now (results/backends-chrome.*, results/backends-firefox.*), so these
# two names are never regenerated, and the run behind them predates the
# correction in that script's header: they report WebGPU as absent on this box,
# when navigator.gpu does resolve a hardware adapter and only does so on a secure
# origin. The per-browser files are the ones to read. Delete these when you no
# longer want the 2026-08-06 numbers on disk.
/results/backends.json
/results/backends.md

# The eight library versions ecosystem/ compares: each is a clone of a GMOD repo
# at a tag pinned in ecosystem/versions.json, carrying its own .git and its own
# node_modules. ecosystem/setup.sh rebuilds them in a few minutes.
ecosystem/.libs/
